# Veritix is built and tested on Linux and on Windows, and testdata/ is data
# rather than source: a CSV whose line endings changed on checkout is a
# different file to audit, and a check that counts something in it would pass
# on one platform and fail on the other for a reason nobody would look for.
# Git for Windows enables core.autocrlf by default, so without this file a
# Windows checkout is not the tree CI measured.
#
# Every text file is stored and checked out with LF, whatever the platform
# does by convention, so a working tree is byte-identical everywhere.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The workbook fixtures are opaque: never normalized, never diffed as text.
# text=auto guesses this correctly today, and saying it means a guess that
# goes the other way cannot corrupt a committed binary on somebody's checkout.
*.xlsx binary
*.xls  binary

# The exception, and it is the one file the LF rule would break: a batch file
# is read by cmd.exe, which is entitled to its own line endings. Checked out
# with CRLF everywhere, including on the Linux machine that never runs it, so
# what goes into a release archive is the same file wherever it was built.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
